====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{senseid|en|change size}} {{lb|en|transitive}} To change the [[size]] of something whilst maintaining [[proportion]]; especially to change a process in order to produce much larger amounts of the final product.
#: {{ux|en|We should '''scale''' that up by a factor of 10.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[climb]] to the top of.
#: {{ux|en|Hilary and Norgay were the first known to have '''scaled''' Everest.}}
#* {{RQ:Burroughs Land That Time Forgot||IX|passage=At last I came to the great barrier-cliffs; and after three days of mad effort—of maniacal effort—I '''scaled''' them. I built crude ladders; I wedged sticks in narrow fissures; I chopped toe-holds and finger-holds with my long knife; but at last I '''scaled''' them. Near the summit I came upon a huge cavern.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1932|author=Dorothy L Sayers|title=Have his Carcase|chapter=1
|passage=A solitary rock is always attractive. All right-minded people feel an overwhelming desire to '''scale''' and sit upon it.}}
#* {{RQ:Roethke Collected|Feud|4|The dead leap at the throat, destroy<br>The meaning of the day; dark forms<br>Have '''scaled''' your walls, and spies betray<br>Old secrets to amorphous swarms.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|business|computing}} To tolerate significant increases in [[throughput]] or other potentially limiting factors.
#: {{ux|en|That architecture won't '''scale''' to real-world environments.}}
# {{sense|computing or systems engineering, of systems}} To handle heavily increasing loads without breaking down.
#* {{Q|en|Sarah Levinger|Post of "X" (formerly "Twitter")|year=May 12, 2025|quote=We started using the term '''“scaling”''' in place of “growing” around the late 1990s to early 2000s, primarily due to the rise of venture-backed startups in Silicon Valley. ''The term migrated from tech/infrastructure (“can this system '''scale'''?”)'' to startups (“can this business '''scale'''?”)}} 
# {{sense|business: entrepreneurship, marketing and venture management, coordinate term to "grow"}} To increase revenues exponentially without proportional increases in costs. 
#* {{Q|en|Sarah Levinger|Post of "X" (formerly "Twitter")|year=May 12, 2025|quote=We started using the term '''“scaling”''' in place of “growing” around the late 1990s to early 2000s, primarily due to the rise of venture-backed startups in Silicon Valley. [...] VCs needed a way ''to describe businesses that could grow exponentially without adding proportional headcount or expense. So '''scalability''' became the holy grail''. The term migrated from tech/infrastructure (''“can this system '''scale'''?”'') to startups (''“can this business '''scale'''?”'') and now everyone from lifestyle brands to DTC businesses use it as a universal term for "make business go big." (By the mid-2010s, '''“scale”''' was in every pitch deck and founder tweet, even if what they really meant was: “We’re trying to 𝘨𝘳𝘰𝘸, but we want to sound smarter so we're using the term "'''scale'''".”) This change in language actually had a HUGE effect on the minds of the people who were operating businesses across the globe.}}
#* {{Q|en|Alexandre Mathieu|Post on "Linked in"|year=2024|quote=Being able to move swiftly, try new things is critical in startups, so there shouldn't be friction upstream, but ''you need to make sure you have good process to purge things that don't '''scale''' with the rest''.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[weigh]], [[measure]] or [[grade]] according to a scale or system.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Coriolanus|II|iii|passage='''Scaling''' his present bearing with his past.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1962|month=July|author=G. Freeman Allen|title=The New "Rheingold"|journal=Modern Railways|page=25|text=The kitchen-dining-buffet car '''scales''' 49.2 tons.}}
# {{lb|en|manufacturing|transitive}} To take measurements from (an {{w|engineering drawing}}), treating them as (or [[as if#Conjunction|as if]]) reliable [[dimension#Noun|dimensional]] instructions. 
#: {{ux|en|Every single drawing in the specification has a warning in its title block which reads, "Do not '''scale''' this drawing."}}

