====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To place a seal on (a document).
# To mark with a stamp, as an evidence of standard exactness, legal size, or merchantable quality.
#: {{ux|en|to '''seal''' weights and measures}}
#: {{ux|en|to '''seal''' silverware}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To fasten (something) so that it cannot be opened without visible [[damage]].
#: {{ux|en|The cover is '''sealed'''. If anyone tries to open it, we'll know about it.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To prevent people or vehicles from [[cross]]ing (something).
#: {{syn|en|block|block off|close|close off|obstruct|seal off}}
#: {{ux|en|The border has been '''sealed''' until the fugitives are found.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To close securely to prevent [[leakage]].
#: {{ux|en|I've '''sealed''' the bottle to keep the contents fresh.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 6-2|I|ii|passage='''Seal''' up your lips, and give no words but "mum".}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To place in a sealed container.
#: {{syn|en|enclose}}
#: {{ux|en|I've '''sealed''' the documents in this envelope.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|chess}} To place a notation of one's next move in a sealed envelope to be opened after an [[adjournment]].
#: {{ux|en|After thinking for half an hour, the champion '''sealed''' his move.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[guarantee]].
#: {{ux|en|The last-minute goal '''sealed''' United’s win.}}
#: {{ux|en|'''seal''' one's destiny}}
#: {{ux|en|'''seal''' someone's fate}}
#: {{ux|en|'''seal''' a job or a contract}}
#* {{quote-web|en|author=w:Phil McNulty| title=[[w:Tunisia national football team|Tunisia]] 1 – 2 [[w:England national football team|England]]| archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20190421215852/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/44434276| archivedate=21 April 2019| work=w:BBC Sport| date=18 June 2018| passage=England's first-half display contained much to admire but it was a sign of their wastefulness in front of goal that it took the injury-time intervention from Kane to '''seal''' victory.}}
# To fix, as a piece of iron in a wall, with [[cement]] or [[plaster]], etc.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1898| title=The American Archaeologist|author=| page=267| passage='''Sealed''' to this wall by their rims were cazuelas ( earthenware bowls).}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1971| title=Environmental Conservation, the Oil and Gas Industries|author=| page=127| passage=After testing is concluded and it has been determined that the drilled well is to be completed as a producing or fluid-injection well, or that operations are to be suspended, the final string of casing is placed in the well and '''sealed''' to the penetrated formation with cement. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1974| title=Ruins Stabilization in the Southwestern United States |author=| page=40| passage=The PVC was then '''sealed''' to the plastered foundation with a 3-inch-wide band of PVC-to-concrete adhesive applied above the Thiokol.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1974| title=Egyptian Journal of Physics - Volumes 5-6 |author=| page=2| passage=The blowing device consists of a glass vessel with a hollow perforated cylinder '''sealed''' to its base, and two side tubes '''sealed''' at opposite ends of the vessel. }}
# To close by means of a seal.
#: {{ux|en|to '''seal''' a drainpipe with water}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2008| title=Conservation and Restoration of Glass |author=Sandra Davison; R.G. Newton | page=301| passage=When the silicone rubber has set the plaster pieces are replaced, followed by the lid, which is '''sealed''' to the mother-mould with plaster and bandage as previously described.}}
# {{lb|en|Mormonism}} To bind eternally as family members.
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Howard Stansbury|year=1852|title=An Expedition to the Valley of the Great Salt Lake of Utah|passage=If a man once married desires a second helpmate {{...}} she is '''sealed''' to him under the solemn sanction of the church.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1870| title=Execution of Laws in Utah|author=Aaron Harrison Cragin | page=9| passage=She can be '''sealed''' to this other man and still remain with her first husband; and the Mormons believe that all her children will belong to the man to whom she is '''sealed'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2001| title=The Mythical West|author=Richard W. Slatta | page=197| passage=Next, I was '''sealed''' to my fourteenth wife, Emeline Vaughn. In 1851, I was '''sealed''' to my fifteenth wife, Mary Lear Groves. In 1856, I was '''sealed''' to my sixteenth wife, Mary Ann Williams.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2010|title=The Latter-day Saint Family Encyclopedia |author=Christopher Kimball Bigelow; Jonathan Langford | page=306| passage=In temples of God, families are eternally '''sealed''' through this power.  Couples who enter into the Covenant of Eternal Marriage are '''sealed''' together, meaning that their union on earth is also valid in the heavens, rather than ending at death.  Children who are subsequently born to them or later '''sealed''' to them are theirs for eternity… In addition, temple sealings are performed by proxy for the dead, with spouses '''sealed''' to each other and children '''sealed''' to parents.}}
# {{lb|en|Christianity}} To form a [[sacred]] [[commitment]].
