===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|sythe}}, {{m|enm|sithe}}, from {{inh|en|ang|sīþe}}, {{m|ang|sīgþe}}, {{m|ang|sigdi|t=sickle}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*sigiþi}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*sigiþiz}}, {{m|gem-pro|*sigiþō}}, derived from {{m|gmw-pro|*seg-|t=saw}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*sek-|t=to cut}}.<ref>{{R:Online_Etymology_Dictionary|scythe}}</ref>

Immediate {{cog|gem|-}} cognates include {{cog|gml|sēgede}}, {{cog|nl|zicht}}, {{cog|is|sigð}} (all “sickle”). More distantly related with {{cog|nl|zeis}}, {{cog|de|Sense}} (both “scythe”). Also akin to {{m+|en|saw}}, which see.

The silent ''c'' crept in during the early 15th century owing to folk-etymological association with {{noncog|la-med|scissor|t=tailor, carver}}, from {{noncog|la|scindō|t=to cut, rend, split}}.

The verb, which was first used in the intransitive sense, is from the noun.

