===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A physical barrier that shelters or hides.
## A physical [[divider]] intended to block an area from view, or provide shelter from something dangerous.
##: {{ux|en|a fire '''screen'''}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Macbeth|V|vi|passage=Your leavy '''screens''' throw down.}}
##* {{RQ:Bacon Essayes|chapter=Of Ambition|passage=There is also great use of ambitious men in being '''screens''' to princes in matters of danger and envy}}
## {{lb|en|architecture}} A dwarf wall or partition carried up to a certain height for separation and protection, as in a church, to separate the aisle from the choir, etc.
## A stand of [[tree]]s or other [[plant]]s that can block [[wind]].
## {{lb|en|military|nautical}} A [[military]] or [[nautical]] formation that blocks or separates; a collection of less-valuable [[vessel]]s that travel with a more valuable one for the latter's protection.
## {{lb|en|figurative}} A [[disguise]]; [[concealment]].
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1987|author=Saul Bellow|title=More Die of Heartbreak
|passage=They'd say he was operating behind a '''screen''' of guilelessness and was a superhypocrite.}}
# A material woven from fine [[wire]]s intended to block animals or large particles from passing while allowing gasses, liquids and finer particles to pass.
## A [[mesh]] layer in a [[window]] or [[doorway]] that lets in fresh air but keeps out pests.
## {{lb|en|mining|quarrying}} A frame supporting a mesh of bars or wires used to classify fragments of stone by size, allowing the passage of fragments whose a diameter is smaller than the distance between the bars or wires.
## {{lb|en|baseball}} The [[protective]] [[netting]] which protects the audience from flying objects
##: {{ux|en|Jones caught the foul up against the '''screen'''.}}
## {{lb|en|printing}} A [[stencil]] upon a framed [[mesh]] through which [[paint]] is forced onto printed-on material; the frame with the mesh itself.
# {{lb|en|by analogy}} Searching through a sample for a target; an act of screening, or the method for it.
#: {{ux|en|a drug '''screen''', a genetic '''screen'''}}
## {{lb|en|genetics}} A technique used to identify genes so as to study gene functions. <!--uncountable??-->
## {{lb|en|countable}} A system intended to separate something into groups based on various criteria.
# A surface on which various forms or formats of information are displayed.
## The [[view]]ing [[surface]] or [[area]] of a movie or of a moving picture or slide presentation.
##* {{RQ:Churchill Celebrity|chapter=1|passage=The stories did not seem to me to touch life. {{...}} They left me with the impression of a well-delivered stereopticon lecture, with characters about as life-like as the shadows on the '''screen''', and whisking on and off, at the mercy of the operator.}}
### {{lb|en|by extension}} A [[room]] in a cinema.
## The informational [[view]]ing [[area]] of [[electronic]] [[device]]s, where [[output]] is displayed.
##* '''1977''', Sex Pistols, ''Spunk'', “Problems” (song):
##*: {{quote|en|You won't find me living for the '''screen''' {{...}} I ain't equipment I ain't automatic}}
## One of the individual regions of a [[video game]], etc. divided into separate screens.
##* '''1988''', Marcus Berkmann, ''Sophistry'' (video game review) in ''Your Sinclair'' issue 30, June 1988
##*: {{quote|en|The idea is to reach the 21st level of an enormous network of interlocking '''screens''', each of which is covered with blocks that you bounce along on.}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1989|title=Compute|volume=11|page=51
|passage=Bub and Bob, the brontosaur buddies, must battle bullies by bursting their bubbles. One or two players can move through 100 '''screens''' of arcade-style graphics.}}
## {{lb|en|computer}} The [[visualise]]d data or imagery displayed on a computer screen.
##: {{ux|en|After you turn on the computer, the login '''screen''' appears.}}
##: {{ux|en|Clicking the Edit button sends you to a '''screen''' where you can change the name and description.}}
## {{lb|en|cricket|by extension}} An erection of white canvas or wood placed on the boundary opposite a batsman to make the ball more easily visible.
# {{n-g|Definitions related to standing in the path of an opposing player}}
## {{lb|en|American football}} {{ellipsis of|en|screen pass}}.
## {{lb|en|basketball}} An offensive [[tactic]] in which a player stands so as to [[block]] a defender from reaching a teammate.
##: {{syn|en|pick}}
# {{lb|en|Scotland|archaic}} A large [[scarf]].

